## Verdict (SPEC §19): **PARTIAL PASS**

Correctness green; some performance gate not met - see table.

### What this verdict does NOT mean (SPEC §20, verbatim)

- No adoption of dependency propagation into GSG, no graph architecture decision,
  no provenance/Observation/Relevance changes, no domain binding, no static/dynamic
  model choice, no parallelization, no scheduler change, no production performance
  budget. FULL PASS means only: experimental evidence suffices to justify moving
  to the next design/functional-feasibility stage within the measured limits.

### Predictions vs outcomes (pre-registered in DESIGN §8 - read AFTER results)

| Prediction | Outcome |
|---|---|
| P-A saturation: union closures 30-70% of N at 1% changes, PERF-3 condition may not hold | **CONFIRMED, stronger**: 99.3% (T1) / 99.6% (T4) - random-DAG future-cone saturates far beyond guess |
| P-B: C2 S9 ratio 1-2x (gate ok); C1 ~E-ratio ~100x fails PERF-4 | **CONFIRMED**: C2 1.35x PASS; C1 256.8x (E-scaled, as predicted direction-wise, worse magnitude) |
| P-C: S2 C2 >=50% easily; C1 modest | **CONFIRMED**: C2 100% (0.006ms vs 33.7ms), C1 72% (9.3 vs 33.7) |
| P-D: S8 both within 3x baseline | **CONFIRMED**: C1 0.56x, C2 0.26x (both FASTER, not slower) |
| P-E: per-change C2 independent of C; batch crossover C4-C7; B2 ~30-50% closure; T4 first C1 regression; B4 index ~= edge mirror | **CONFIRMED with refinement**: per-change c2/c0 = 0.11-0.14 constant-ish; batch crossover at cc=40 (between 4 and 40); B2: benefit dies when union closure ~100% (measured: any uniform change >=0.1% saturates T1); C1 slower than C0 on T4 at 1% (S5 1.38x) and on T3 (S3 1.13x, S6 1.29x) and batch cc>=40 (1.4-1.6x); B4: reverse index = 24B/dep vs edge-array 51B/dep (about half), peak WS +2-4% only |

### Candidate comparison (SPEC §16)

| Dimension | C1 affected-set sweep | C2 indexed BFS |
|---|---|---|
| S2 single local change | 9.3ms (72% faster than C0) | 0.006ms (100%) |
| S9 locality (100K/1K) | 256.8x (FAIL PERF-4) | 1.35x (PASS) |
| Dense T4 @1% | 1.38x SLOWER than C0 | 0.53x (2x faster) |
| Hub T3 | 1.13x slower | 0.70x faster |
| High density (S8) | 0.56x | 0.26x |
| Batch cc>=40 | 1.4-1.6x slower | 1.5-1.9x slower |
| Memory (fixed reader) | ~1.00x baseline | 1.02-1.04x baseline (+24B/dep) |
| Implementation complexity | one flat sweep, no extra structure | reverse CSR + BFS stack |

**P1-COMP verdict:** C2 dominates C1 materially on every dimension that matters
(locality, dense, hub, high-density) at a negligible memory cost (+2-4% WS,
24B/dep vs 51B/dep for the shared edge array). C1's only advantage is structural
simplicity (no second structure), but it FAILS the locality gate (PERF-4) and is
slower than full recompute on dense/hub/batch regimes. P1-COMP-1 (prefer faster)
applies; P1-COMP-4 (index must justify itself) is answered: it does.

### Break-even characterization (SPEC §15 - measured, not imposed)

- **B1 (change density):** under per-change cadence on T1@40K, C2 stays 6-12x
  faster than C0 at every measured density (0.1%, 5%, 20%) - no crossover. C1
  crosses at cc=40 (0.1%): 0.41x at cc=40 but 1.42x by batch cc=40 equivalent.
  Under batch cadence, BOTH cross between cc=4 (0.51-0.75x) and cc=40 (1.42-1.53x).
- **B2 (affected work):** benefit disappears when union closure saturates N. On
  random T1 DAGs, ANY uniform change of >=0.1% of N saturates ~97-100%. The A-level
  ladder shows per-change closures collapse the advantage only when a single change's
  own closure is large (S3 14.6K, S5 39.9K).
- **B3 (topology):** C1 inverts (slower than baseline) on T3 hub (1.13-1.29x) and
  T4 dense (1.38x) - the O(E) sweep cost exceeds full O(N) evaluation when
  E/N is high (T3: 20.8, T4: 20.0) and the closure is near-N anyway. C2 never
  inverted in any measured scenario.
- **B4 (memory):** reverse index = 24B/dependency (about half the 51B/dep shared
  edge array). Peak WS impact measured at only +2.0-3.6% (fixed reader). No
  memory pathology at any measured scale.

### Failure/boundary analysis (SPEC §18.9)

- No crashes, no OOM, no integrity failures across 156 isolated subprocess runs
  (78 matrix x 2 passes) + 42 memory-rerun runs.
- One measurement-infrastructure defect found & fixed mid-run: psapi
  GetProcessMemoryInfo silently returned 0 (ERROR_INSUFFICIENT_BUFFER) with the
  10-field struct; replaced by kernel32 K32GetProcessMemoryInfo with the full
  PROCESS_MEMORY_COUNTERS_EX (11 fields) + argtypes; memory gates re-measured in
  isolated reruns. Timing hashes/tables unaffected (memory read happens after
  timers).
- One harness bug found & fixed before any matrix data was kept: per-candidate
  seed divergence (order was per-run, not per-scenario) caused cross-candidate
  world mismatch; fixed to per-scenario order and the whole matrix was re-run
  from scratch (polluted results deleted, ~50 min lost - documented, not hidden).
- PERF-3 N/A: the condition (closure <= 10% of N at 1% uniform changes) is
  unreachable on random T1 DAGs - 1% uniform changes give 99.3% union closure.
  The gate is honestly recorded as condition-not-met with the actual measured
  speedup (87.4%) presented alongside.

